load @term/seed/structure/manifold
  find manifold

# Geodesics in hyperbolic space, the straight lines of the curved geometry, the shortest paths and the paths of no
# turning. Their behavior is the visible signature of negative curvature, and it is unlike anything in flat space.

# A geodesic: the locally-shortest path between points, the curve a taut string would follow, equivalently the curve
# whose acceleration has no component along the surface (zero geodesic curvature). In the Poincare disk a geodesic is a
# circular arc meeting the boundary at right angles or a diameter; in the upper half-space a vertical line or a
# semicircle centered on the boundary; in the Klein model a straight chord. The form carries the manifold it runs in.
form geodesic
  link manifold, like manifold

# Limiting (asymptotic) parallels: two geodesics that never meet yet approach each other, sharing a single ideal point
# on the boundary. Through a point off a line there are exactly TWO limiting parallels, one for each end of the line,
# and they bound the angle of parallelism. These are the "parallels" of the hyperbolic parallel axiom.
form limiting-parallel
  link manifold, like manifold

# Ultraparallel (diverging) geodesics: two geodesics that neither meet nor share an ideal point, drifting apart
# exponentially in both directions. Any two ultraparallel lines have a UNIQUE common perpendicular, the segment of least
# distance between them, a feature with no Euclidean analogue. Through a point off a line there are infinitely many
# ultraparallels (every line between the two limiting parallels).
form ultraparallel
  link manifold, like manifold

# Geodesic divergence: two geodesics from one point spread apart so that the distance between them grows EXPONENTIALLY
# with arc length, the hyperbolic-sine law. This exponential spreading is why a small region commands an astronomically
# large frontier a few steps out, the exponential workforce the substrate and Margenstern's machines spend.
form geodesic-divergence
  link manifold, like manifold
